    "id": 770,
    "name": "Notatesseraeraptor",
    "temporalRange": "Late Triassic, ~209 Ma",
    "diet": "carnivore",
    "locomotionType": "biped",
    "description": "Notatesseraeraptor (\"feature mosaic tile thief\"; from the Latin \"nota\", feature; \"tesserae\", tiles used to make a mosaic, in reference to the mixture of features normally found on dilophosaurids and coelophysoids; and \"raptor\", thief) is a genus of carnivorous theropod dinosaur that lived during the Late Triassic of what is now Switzerland. It was found in the Gruhalde Member of the Klettgau Formation. It was an early member of Neotheropoda with affinities to Dilophosaurus and Averostra. The new genus and species Notatesseraeraptor frickensis was named by Marion Zahner and colleagues in 2019.[1]",
