    "temporalRange": "Early Maastrichtian, 70.6–70 Ma",
    "diet": "herbivore",
    "locomotionType": "quadruped",
    "description": "Arrhinoceratops (meaning \"no nose-horn face\", derived from the Ancient Greek \"a-/α-\" \"no\", rhis/ῥίς \"nose\" \"keras/κέρας\" \"horn\", \"-ops/ὤψ\" \"face\") is a genus of herbivorous ceratopsian dinosaur. The name was coined as its original describer concluded it was special because the nose-horn was not a separate bone, however further analysis revealed this was based on a misunderstanding.[1] It lived during the latest Campanian/earliest Maastrichtian stage of the Late Cretaceous, predating its famous relative Triceratops by a few million years, although it was contemporary with Anchiceratops.[1] Its remains have been found in Canada.[1]",
