    "id": 313,
    "name": "Diplodocus",
    "temporalRange": "Late Jurassic (Kimmeridgian), 154–152 Ma",
    "diet": "herbivore",
    "locomotionType": "biped",
    "description": "Diplodocus (/dɪˈplɒdəkəs/,[3][4] /daɪˈplɒdəkəs/,[4] or /ˌdɪploʊˈdoʊkəs/[3]) was a genus of diplodocid sauropod dinosaurs, whose fossils were first discovered in 1877 by S. W. Williston. The generic name, coined by Othniel Charles Marsh in 1878, is a Neo-Latin term derived from Greek διπλός (diplos) \"double\" and δοκός (dokos) \"beam\",[3][5] in reference to the double-beamed chevron bones located in the underside of the tail, which were then considered unique.",
